As they often do, if an editor of a big corporate media house had been in my #position, one of the industry's# editors might have written a story about two #politicians-#Vijay and #PrashantKishor who struggled in 2025 but eventually achieved great success in 2026. They might then have approached these politicians to seek favours for their media organisation and other business interests of their owners.

Unfortunately, this has become a common practice among editors, television channel heads, print media executives and owners of major corporate media houses.

Well, If you are wondering whether any #holy book says that only a few leaders possess #political skills, or that only a select few people with particular #surnames have political abilities, then you are mistaken.

I have been following politics since 1995, and based on my own political #knowledge, #skills, and #judgement, I wrote a story about #Vijay and #PrashantKishor, despite both having faced challenging years in 2025. I published the story under the title “Emerging Politicians in 2025” through #NationalPoliticalMirror, the #world's only #weekly political magazine and an independent media house.

If any other editor had been in my position, that editor and their team might have met these politicians and sought their support for their news organisation, as well as assistance from the owners of the big corporate media houses they represent.

However, I never did that, and I will never do so, no matter how much I have to struggle or how many days I have to work hard to meet my daily needs and support National Political Mirror.

Instead, I called one of these politicians and requested a meeting. He told me that he would meet me whenever he was in Delhi. However, that meeting never took place, and I neither followed up with him nor repeatedly asked him to meet me.

Well, ladies and gentlemen, putting aside my political skills, I am giving you a recent example of why no big corporate media house can ever match the content and honesty of National Political Mirror (and I will provide you with more examples later).

In 2025, I highlighted Joseph Vijay Chandrasekhar from South India, who founded the political party Tamizhaga Vettri Kazhagam (TVK), and Prashant Kishor (PK) from North India, who founded the Jan Suraaj Party (JSP), in my cover story, “#EmergingPoliticians in 2025,” published in an edition of National Political Mirror. Both of my predictions have now come true.

The Congress has supported Vijay’s political ambition to become the Chief Minister of #TamilNadu. Similarly, senior Congress leader and former MP #NikhilKumar reportedly expressed his support for Jan Suraaj Party founder Prashant Kishor during the #Bankipur Assembly by-election.

Besides Nikhil Kumar’s reported support, there has also been indirect support from #RahulGandhi for Prashant Kishor. Prashant Kishor was elected as an MLA on August 3. Although he is not the Chief Minister, many people in Bihar are already viewing him as a potential Chief Minister, despite the fact that his party, the Jan Suraaj Party, has only one MLA—and that MLA is Prashant Kishor.

Lastly, I said in my story for the December 28, 2025 edition of National Political Mirror, the world’s only weekly political magazine and India’s sole independent media house: “And, believe me, I have no evidence, but I am convinced that Prashant Kishor and Vijay have tremendous support from prominent Congress leaders and India’s Opposition Leader, #RahulGandhi.”

