Videos are increasingly appearing on social media featuring Canadian citizens speaking from Cuba about the humanitarian crisis facing the Cuban people. They claim to understand the harsh reality that we Cubans have endured for more than six decades, yet they offer their commentary from the comfort of a hotel, a beach, surrounded by beachside restaurants, music, cigars and rum.

From that setting, they lecture others about how necessary it is for the United States to lift its sanctions against the Cuban regime, arguing that these measures hurt the Cuban people more than the system in power.

Their entire narrative places the blame for Cuba’s current humanitarian crisis on a foreign country. Yet not once do they seriously acknowledge the real source of the problem: the Cuban regime itself, which has controlled every aspect of the country for nearly seven decades.

Since coming to power, the regime has received enormous amounts of financial assistance and resources, enough to have built a prosperous country. Just look at Europe after World War II. Through the Marshall Plan, European countries managed to rebuild their economies and recover from devastation. Meanwhile, the Cuban regime received substantial economic support from the Soviet Union for decades, followed by billions in assistance from Venezuela. In many ways, Cuba received its own versions of the Marshall Plan more than once.

So, where did all that money and assistance go? Why, after so many decades and so much external support, is Cuba still in ruins?

What is particularly disturbing is watching people who have never experienced communism firsthand, who have never lived under its consequences, speak with such certainty about a reality they simply do not know. From the comfort of tourist resorts, they repeat the Cuban regime’s propaganda, blame a foreign nation and conveniently ignore those who have actually suffered under this system.

You can visit Cuba, enjoy its beaches, hotels, music, cigars and rum, and still know absolutely nothing about what it means to live as an ordinary Cuban.

There is a difference between visiting a country and understanding the reality of its people. And when you use your platform to defend the narrative of those responsible for their suffering, you are no longer simply expressing an opinion. You are becoming complicit in their misery.
